[LIMITING] the intensity scale varies by 78% within a run (log range 0.58); a smooth per-run scale cuts R_int from 15.7% to 8.2%  (intensity_drift)
  measured : log_scale_range_per_run = {'r02': 0.365, 'r03': 0.467, 'r04': 0.578}; rint_run_scales_only = 0.1567; rint_smooth_scale = 0.0819; rint_gain = 0.477; rint_permuted_frames = 0.1531; placebo_gain = 0.023; k_over_frame_bins = {'r02': [0.903, 0.835, 0.827, 0.932, 1.08, 1.139, 1.15, 1.133], 'r03': [1.117, 1.164, 1.148, 0.945, 0.797, 0.876, 0.964, 0.989], 'r04': [1.197, 0.988, 0.795, 0.758, 0.873, 1.028, 1.165, 1.196]}; i0_recorded = False
  rule     : drift when the log scale range >= 0.15 and the R_int gain >= 25%, and the permuted-frame placebo gains less than 50% of the real gain (max-min of the fitted log scale within a run; +-7 % or more over a scan is a visible drift (L-alanine: 0.37-0.58); relative R_int reduction by the smooth scale; below this the scale is not doing anything the data need)
  evidence : merge.scale_smooth (8 knots per run over the frame index, fitted from equivalents only) against merge.scale_groups; placebo = the same fit with the frame labels permuted within each run (5 seeds)
  do       : The scale is INFERRED from equivalents, not measured: check that the crystal is centred on the rotation axis for the whole rotation and that the beam covers (or is smaller than) the crystal consistently; record the incident intensity (and, if possible, the transmitted beam) for every frame so the scale can be measured; if the crystal is much larger than the beam, a centring error shows up as exactly this kind of slow variation.
  gain     : on THIS data a smooth scale lowers R_int from 15.7% to 8.2% (in sample; placebo with permuted frames: 15.3%); a held-out check is real/a39_cv_robust.py in the validation directory
  not covered: the scale absorbs anything smooth in the frame index that multiplies a whole run (beam or sample-volume drift, centring, absorption by a symmetric environment); it cannot say which. Needs equivalents spread over each run's whole range

